WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.320
Film Courage: What 5 things does a screenplay
have to have for you to be interested in it

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.320
as a producer?

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.840
Mark Heidelberger, Producer: What 5 things?

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:15.660
I never thought about breaking it down to
5 things.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:22.380
First and foremost for me, the script has
to be (in my eyes) well-written.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:29.890
It’s got to be a story that…I don’t
care what the story is in terms of genre.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:33.440
You know people ask me “Do you care about
a certain genre?

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.090
You seem to work in all certain genres.”

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.480
Because to me good story transcends genre,
it has nothing to do with genre.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.129
Genre is just a set of conventions that allow
me to identify what a movie is and therefore

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:44.730
whether they want to see it or not.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.470
Genre has nothing to do with whether you can
tell a good story in any genre.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.980
And to me working with different genres is
part of the excitement of working in this

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:51.980
business.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.140
That’s why so many of us love being in this
business, right?

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.999
We don’t go to the same office every day
and we’re always pursuing something different

00:00:56.999 --> 00:01:00.399
and so having different genres to work in
is great.

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:02.390
And so for me, it’s just a well-told story.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:06.720
Is there some sort of character, some sort
of protagonist that you identify with his

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.720
journey in some way?

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.000
I’m going to use the word likable.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.251
I’m just going to say is it a protagonist
I can identify with in some way?

00:01:12.251 --> 00:01:15.080
I want to follow him for some reason.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.190
You know, does he have goals, does he have
flaws, is there an antagonist that is creating

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.250
conflict with this guy or girl or whatever
the situation is.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:28.060
But is there something I can get behind and
is there a strong theme?

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.340
Like why are you telling this story?

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.630
So I mean just in terms of just being well-written.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.810
And then I would say Number 2 is then what
is the budget range and is there a budget

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.840
range that’s reasonable?

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.500
So a lot of people they come to me and I get
a lot of “How much would it cost to do this

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.500
script?”

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.810
And I’m like “Okay, well I’ll answer
that question if you can answer a question

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:50.810
for me.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:51.810
How long is a piece of string?”

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.460
That’s what I like to ask them.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.000
You can’t just give me a script…how much
does it cost to make?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.430
Is Matt Damon in your movie?

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.430
Your budget just went up by 10 million dollars.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:03.060
I mean I can make an argument to do this movie
for a million bucks or ten million bucks.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.450
So really it’s more like how much can you
raise?

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.880
Or how much do you have?

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.551
Or if they really don’t have a good idea
of this - who do you see in it, how wide do

00:02:12.551 --> 00:02:13.551
you see it being distributed?

00:02:13.551 --> 00:02:19.100
But I have to get an idea of what range and
how big they’re seeing this in their head.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:24.860
And a lot of times I might read the script
and go well “That’s not going to happen

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:25.860
on this.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:31.250
You’re not going to do Avatar on a $500,000
budget” or whatever?

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:38.230
So it has to be doable for the budget.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.940
And beyond that I guess logistically feasible.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:48.930
For me sometimes I look and go “Yeah the
budget’s okay.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.810
It’s possible.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.620
But I think for some other logistical reason,
it would be very challenging to pull this

00:02:53.620 --> 00:03:01.290
off even if budget-wise it can be done, there
might be something else that would preclude

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:07.520
it from being…or at least allow me to do
it effectively for whatever that reason is.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:13.040
But think those are really the two main things
that I look for, is the budget reasonable

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.480
for the script and is the script well-written?

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.080
And beyond that, do I like working with this
person (do I get a good sense)?

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.319
Because you’re going to spend a year or
more dealing with this person depending on

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:31.700
the scope of the duties.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.640
So sometimes I’m just literally brought
on to…there’s no development and I’m

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.370
like “Okay, the script is decent.”

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:43.090
And I think I can influence it enough through
the production process to make it a good picture.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.390
And then at post when I deliver the master
drive or the DCP or whatever the final deliverable

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.480
is, I’m done.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.400
I’m not involved in marketing or distribution.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.510
There are projects like that (it just depends
on the term of the contract).

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.849
But I would say at the least I’m spending
at least a year with this person.

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:05.710
So a well-written script, reasonable budget
and logistics and I like the person.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:06.710
Those are really my main criteria.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.510
I don’t think I can get to five.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.620
Film Courage: Those are three, right?

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.090
Mark Heidelberger: There can be sub-factors
within there, but those are sort of broad

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.030
scope factors.

